        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "Example 2.12. If X ⊂ Rn is closed, we have M(X) ⊂ Æ(X), by noting for that each µ ∈ M(X), the mapping φ  → X φdµ is well-deﬁned and weakly∗-continuous on Lipb(X). However this space is strictly larger in general; if a ∈ X is an accumulation point of X, then we can ﬁnd a sequence (ak)k ⊂ X converging to a such that ak = a for all k. By passing to a subsequence if necessary, assume that k|ak − a| < ∞. Then m = ∞k=1(δa"
